@model VoteButtonModel
@{
    // The button carries an explicit intent instead of toggling, so a repeated submission
    // asks the server for the same end state rather than undoing the previous one.
    string handler = VotingPageModel.VoteHandlerName;
    string label = UiText.VoteButton;

    if (Model.HasVoted)
    {
        handler = VotingPageModel.UnvoteHandlerName;
        label = UiText.VotedButton;
    }

    string buttonId = $"voto-{Model.IdeaId}";
    string titleId = $"ideia-titulo-{Model.IdeaId}";

    // Two states the stylesheet needs and cannot work out on its own, because neither of
    // them can be read off the wording: CSS cannot look at the text of the count to find
    // out it says none, and the voted state has to be legible as more than a word.
    string formClasses = "ideia-voto";
    string buttonClasses = string.Empty;

    if (Model.VoteCount <= 0)
    {
        formClasses = "ideia-voto sem-votos";
    }

    if (Model.HasVoted)
    {
        buttonClasses = "votado";
    }
}
<form class="@formClasses" method="post" data-vote-form data-idea-id="@Model.IdeaId" asp-page-handler="@handler">
    <input type="hidden" name="ideaId" value="@Model.IdeaId" />

    @*
        Announcing the new count after a vote is required by the Accessibility section of
        Docs/02, and the attribute below is only half of what makes it happen.

        A live region is announced when the content of a node that was ALREADY in the
        document changes. The enhancement script satisfies that by writing into the
        textContent of this very span, which is why it reads the wording out of the
        server's answer instead of rebuilding the form around it.

        Replace that script with one that re-renders this markup, and the region stops
        announcing while every visible thing stays correct: the attribute is still here,
        the count on screen is still right, and nothing errors. There is no way to see
        this break, so it is written down instead.

        It comes before the button because that is the order it is read in and the order it
        is laid out in: how much support this idea has, then what you can do about it.
    *@
    <span data-vote-count aria-live="polite">@UiText.FormatVoteCount(Model.VoteCount)</span>

    @*
        The spoken name of this button is its own wording followed by the title of the idea
        it votes on, because on the ranking every one of these buttons reads as the same
        single word. Somebody moving through the page button by button would hear "Votar"
        once per idea with nothing to tell them apart, while somebody looking at the screen
        has the heading directly above and never meets the problem. That is what makes this
        one worth guarding: it cannot be caught by looking.

        Both pages that render this partial put the id named here on their title element.
        The button names itself first so that its visible wording stays the beginning of the
        spoken name, which is what keeps the two the same thing. If a future caller renders
        the partial with no such element, the missing reference is ignored and the name falls
        back to the wording alone, which is where this started.
    *@
    <button type="submit"
            class="@buttonClasses"
            id="@buttonId"
            data-vote-button
            aria-labelledby="@buttonId @titleId">@label</button>
</form>